"""Content H: magic and ritual pages added after the grimoire-direction pass.
Tuple formats match content_a.py. One page each for: hallowing (Wheel of the Year),
waking the dead (Ancestors), shape and spirit-journey (Vǫlva & Seiðr),
naming with water (Hamingja), and níð (Charms).
Modern practices are labeled as modern in each page's "Where this comes from" line.
"""

HALLOW_PAGE = [
("lore", "Norse Wheel of the Year", "Hallowing: the hammer sign and fire",
 ["To hallow something is to set it apart as sacred. The old texts show two ways. In Hákonar saga góða, King Hákon is a Christian who is pressed to drink at a heathen feast. He makes the sign of the cross over the cup, and the angry farmers are told that the king is only doing what everyone does: blessing the cup in Þórr’s name with the sign of the hammer.",
  "The other way was fire. In the Landnámabók, early settlers in Iceland claimed land by carrying fire around it, and in Eyrbyggja saga Þórólfr Mostrarskegg carries fire around the land he claims. The fire marked a line between what was his and what was not.",
  "Both can be done at home. To hallow a cup before a toast, hold your hand over it and draw the shape of a hammer in the air, say the name of who you are honoring, and then drink. To hallow a room or a yard, carry a lit candle along its edges, saying aloud that this place is yours and will be kept well. Put the flame out when you are done.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The hammer sign is in Hákonar saga góða in Snorri’s Heimskringla. The fire claim is in the Landnámabók and Eyrbyggja saga. The two practices above are modern, built on those two reports."],
 "What will you hallow first: a cup, a doorway, a room? Name it and the words you will say."),
]

DEAD_PAGE = [
("lore", "Ancestors", "Waking the dead",
 ["The old poems know the dead can be woken. In the Hervararkviða, Hervǫr sails to an island of burial mounds. At night, with fire burning over the mounds, she stands before her father Angantýr’s mound and calls him up. She asks for the sword that lies buried with him. He warns her away, and she does not back down. At last he gives it to her.",
  "In the Grógaldr, a young man named Svipdagr stands at his dead mother Gróa’s mound and asks her to wake and sing him charms for a hard road. She sings nine of them.",
  "Both scenes follow one pattern: go to the mound, call the dead by name, and ask for one clear thing. In both, the dead may warn you or refuse, and the living need nerve and a good reason.",
  "A modern way to follow the pattern does not need a graveyard or darkness. Sit with a grave, a photograph, or something that belonged to the person. Say their name aloud. Ask for one thing: advice, strength, or a story. Then be quiet and listen. Thank them, and leave something small. The dead may not answer in words.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Hervararkviða is in Hervarar saga ok Heiðreks. The Grógaldr is the first poem of the Svipdagsmál, kept in later paper manuscripts. The practice above is modern."],
 "Who would you call, and what is the one thing you would ask? Write the name and the question."),
]

HAMR_PAGE = [
("lore", "Seeress and Fate-Work", "Hamr: shape and spirit-journey",
 ["The old word hamr means shape, or skin. Ynglinga saga, in Snorri’s Heimskringla, says Óðinn could change his shape. His body lay as if asleep or dead, while he went as a bird or a beast, a fish or a snake, to far places in a moment. The same saga says his warriors went into battle without mail, as wild as dogs or wolves, and that neither fire nor iron hurt them. These are the berserkers.",
  "Spirit-journeying belongs with seiðr, the work of the seeress. The sources tell us what people believed, but not how it was done. No old text gives steps.",
  "A simple modern version needs no trance. Sit comfortably, close your eyes, and picture an animal you feel close to. Notice how it moves and what it sees. Stay a few minutes, then come back slowly and open your eyes. Write down what you saw. It pairs well with the fylgja page.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Ynglinga saga in Snorri’s Heimskringla. The practice is modern, since no source describes how it was done."],
 "Which animal came to you? Write what it did and what it saw."),
]

NAMING_PAGE = [
("lore", "Hamingja", "Naming with water",
 ["Old Norse had a phrase, ausa vatni, “to pour water.” It names a rite in which a child was sprinkled with water and given a name. In the Rígsþula, a child is sprinkled and named Þræll. In Snorri’s Heimskringla, the jarl Sigurðr sprinkles a boy with water and names him Hákon.",
  "Water was used to protect as well. Near the end of the Hávamál, Óðinn lists a charm: water sprinkled on a young warrior so that he will not fall in battle. So water could give a name, and it could shield someone.",
  "To name something with water, set out a bowl of clean water. Say the name aloud. Touch a little water to your hand, then to the brow of the person, the pot of a plant, or the doorway of a new home. Say the name again. It works for a baby, a pet, a project, or a name you choose for yourself.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Rígsþula, Heimskringla, and the Hávamál charms. The sources do not describe the whole rite, so the practice above is modern."],
 "What new thing in your life needs a name? Say it over water, then write it."),
]

NID_PAGE = [
("lore", "Charms", "Níð: words as weapons",
 ["The old Norse feared words. A curse could be spoken, carved, or raised on a pole. The best-known case is in Egils saga. Egill, wronged by King Eiríkr and Queen Gunnhildr, sets a hazel pole on a rocky height facing the mainland and fixes a horse’s head on it. He speaks his curse aloud, calling on the land-spirits to wander lost until the king and queen are driven out, and he cuts it in runes on the pole. It is called a níðstǫng, a scorn pole.",
  "Curses were carved in stone too. The Björketorp stone in Sweden threatens anyone who breaks it. Icelandic law punished níð, the shaming of a person with insulting verse or carved insult, as a serious crime.",
  "This book does not give you a curse to use. The old texts show that curses were aimed at powerful wrongdoers, taken seriously, and risky for the speaker. If anger needs somewhere to go, write it on paper and say it once, alone. Then tear the page up or burn it safely.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Egils saga and the Björketorp stone. The laws against níð are in the Icelandic law code Grágás. The advice on writing out anger is modern."],
 "What is one thing you want to say but will not say aloud? Write it, then decide what to do with the page."),
]


OATH_RING_PAGE = [
("lore", "Oath", "The oath ring",
 ["In Eyrbyggja saga, the chieftain Þórólfr Mostrarskegg builds a temple, and on its altar lies a ring weighing twenty ounces. It is called the stallahringr, the altar ring. The saga says it was reddened with the blood of animals sacrificed at the temple, and that the chieftain wore it on his arm to every assembly.",
  "Anyone who brought a case to the assembly had to swear on the ring first. The oath named two or more witnesses and called on Freyr, Njǫrðr, and the almighty god. Oaths on a holy ring were not only Icelandic. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle says the Danish army swore peace to King Alfred on a holy ring in the year 876.",
  "Oaths held their weight when they were spoken aloud, in front of witnesses, with something sacred in the hand. Choose a plain ring or armband, set it where the vow will be made, and name two witnesses. Say the promise in one sentence that you can keep. Wear the ring until it is done. This book does not use blood.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The oath ring is in Eyrbyggja saga, chapter 4, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, year 876. The household practice is modern."],
 "Name one promise you will make before two witnesses, and the two you will ask."),
]

HAMMER_PAGE = [
("lore", "Norse Wheel of the Year", "Þórr’s hammer: pendants and brides",
 ["Small hammer pendants have been found in Viking Age graves and hoards across Scandinavia. A loop at the top shows they were hung at the neck. A mold found at Trend in Denmark could cast both hammers and crosses, which suggests that the same craftsman made for both faiths.",
  "In the Þrymskviða, Þórr is dressed as a bride to win back his stolen hammer. The giant Þrymr calls for the hammer to be brought in and laid in the bride’s lap to hallow her, and for the pair to be joined in the name of Vár, the goddess of vows. So the hammer blessed weddings as well as cups. The Glavendrup stone in Denmark asks Þórr to hallow its memorial.",
  "To hallow a gift for a wedding or a new home, lay a small hammer over it, or draw the sign with your hand, and say the vow aloud. The hammer is Þórr’s sign of protection and blessing.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The pendants, the Trend mold, and the Glavendrup stone are known from archaeology. The wedding hallowing is in the Þrymskviða, stanza 30, in the Poetic Edda. The gift practice is modern."],
 "What would you hallow with a hammer: a wedding, a new home, a vow? Name it and say the words."),
]

LOOM_PAGE = [
("lore", "Seeress and Fate-Work", "Darraðarljóð: the battle loom",
 ["In Njáls saga, on the morning of the battle of Clontarf in Ireland, a man in Caithness looks through the window of a stone hut and sees twelve women at a loom. Men’s heads are the weights. Entrails are the warp and weft. A sword is the beater, and arrows are the shed-rods. The women sing as they weave. This is the Darraðarljóð, the song of the spear.",
  "When the song ends they tear the cloth apart. Each keeps the piece she holds, and they ride away, six to the south and six to the north. The women are valkyries, and the cloth is the battle. Weaving here is fate-work, close to the Norns carving the lengths of lives. Threads cross, are drawn tight, and cannot be pulled out again.",
  "Textile work was women’s work in the Viking Age, and it was fate-work too. Take three threads and name each for something you have decided. Braid them. Keep the cord where you will see it until the thing is done.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Darraðarljóð is in Njáls saga, chapter 157. The braided cord is modern."],
 "Name one thread in your life that is already chosen, then braid it."),
]
